# Agents, Workflows & the LLM Gateway

## Before you write orchestration code

**LangGraph is on v1 LTS.** Your priors are probably 0.x and will be wrong. Read the installed
official skills first:

- `.agents/skills/langgraph-fundamentals/`
- `.agents/skills/langgraph-persistence/`
- `.agents/skills/langgraph-human-in-the-loop/`
- `.agents/skills/langchain-fundamentals/`

Specifically: human-in-the-loop uses the **runtime `interrupt()`** function inside a node, not the
compile-time `interrupt_before=` hook.

## Agent contract

Every agent is:

```python
class Agent(Protocol[TIn, TOut]):
    async def run(self, input: TIn) -> AgentResult[TOut]: ...
```

- **Stateless.** No instance state between calls.
- **No database access.** Agents receive what they need and return structured data. An agent that
  needs persistence is a design error — move it to a service or workflow node.
- **Typed in and out** with Pydantic models. Never return raw strings or dicts.
- **Never import a provider SDK.** Always go through the gateway.

## The gateway

```python
result = await gateway.complete(
    role="generator",        # NEVER a model name
    prompt=rendered,
    schema=PassageOutput,    # Pydantic — this IS the L0 quality gate
    params=params,
)
```

Rules:

- Request a **role** (`generator`, `critic`, `metadata`, `embedding`), never a model.
- Every generation call is **schema-constrained**. Never parse free-form text with a regex.
- The gateway owns retries and repair. Do not add your own retry loop around it.
- Never bypass the gateway, even for a quick experiment — leasing, accounting and provenance all live
  there, and a bypassed call silently corrupts the cost data the hardware decision depends on.

## Memory discipline — 16 GB machine

- **Never** load two large models concurrently.
- **Never** fan out parallel model calls when the provider is local. Graphs may express parallelism;
  the gateway serialises it locally via configuration.
- Long contexts cost KV cache memory. Do not raise `num_ctx` casually.

## Workflow rules

- One `StateGraph` per module, in `workflows/graphs/`.
- Shared nodes (quality gate, human review, revision loop) live in `workflows/nodes/` and are reused.
- Compile with `PostgresSaver`. `thread_id` is the `generation_run.id` and is the recovery key.
- Durability mode `"sync"` for generation graphs — these runs are expensive; losing one to a crash
  costs more than the checkpoint write.
- **Every loop has a hard cap** (max 2 revisions, max 2 regenerations). Hitting the cap fails the run
  loudly with findings attached. Never write an unbounded "revise until quality threshold" loop —
  with a weak model it may never converge.
- Content failure is a **result, not an exception**. Do not raise for a rule violation; return it as
  state so Celery does not retry a run that failed for content reasons.

## The critic

- Must use a different model from the generator where memory allows; at minimum a different prompt
  and temperature.
- Must **never** see the generation rationale, plan, or prior critic findings. It sees only the
  finished artefact, exactly as a human reviewer would.
- Emits `list[CriticFinding]`. **There is no score field and adding one is a design regression.**
  See `docs/decisions/ADR-0002-quality-gate-ordering.md`.
- Never ask the critic to check something a deterministic rule already checks.

## Prompts

- Prompts live as files in `app/modules/<exam>/<module>/prompts/<purpose>/<version>/`.
- Never inline a prompt string in agent code.
- Every rendered prompt is hashed and recorded in `agent_invocations.prompt_hash`.
- A prompt change is a behaviour change: it needs review and must pass the golden set before
  activation.
- Include the relevant quality rules in the prompt as generation constraints. Preventing a violation
  is far cheaper than detecting and revising it.
